From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 20:08:30 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] media: radio: mt6797: make it a V4L2 radio receiver

The bring-up probe tunes the receiver and reads its signal strength, and the
headset cable is its antenna. Turn it into a radio device.

The first open of /dev/radioN attaches to the STP link, switches the WMT FM
function on and runs the full vendor power-up with the identity check, the
DSP patch and coefficient downloads and the last audio step; the last close
runs the vendor power-down. The probe no longer touches the receiver and the
bring-up module parameters are gone.

Supported: one FM band, 87.5-108 MHz in 100 kHz steps; frequency; signal
strength from RSSI, -111 to -51 dBm; the stereo indicator and forced mono;
mute, volume and de-emphasis, 50 or 75 us, 75 by default; and a hardware
seek. The chip's own seek is compiled out in the vendor driver, so the seek
steps through the band asking the firmware for each channel's quality report
and judges it with the vendor's thresholds, then tunes the first good one.

RDS needs no polling: the firmware pushes raw groups as unsolicited events.
They are queued as v4l2_rds_data blocks for read(), with the error flag from
the per-block CRC result and C' for version B groups.

The nine channels that need the 64 MHz common SPI clock are still refused,
and seek skips them. The audio stays on the connectivity subsystem's internal
I2S; nothing routes it to the sound card yet.
